WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:02.900
In this lesson you'll learn

2
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.269
the difference between an AI model,

3
00:00:05.269 --> 00:00:08.334
the app you use it in, and an agent.

4
00:00:08.334 --> 00:00:09.936
The model is the engine,

5
00:00:09.936 --> 00:00:12.200
the app is the car built around it,

6
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.066
and an agent is that same car, allowed to drive,

7
00:00:16.066 --> 00:00:18.225
with you in the passenger seat.

8
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:19.839
Know which one you're dealing with,

9
00:00:19.839 --> 00:00:23.683
and most of AI's surprises start to make sense.

10
00:00:24.134 --> 00:00:25.214
On the course map,

11
00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:28.836
this lesson sits at the Start, in Orientation:

12
00:00:28.836 --> 00:00:31.924
what this course is, and the lantern you carry.

13
00:00:31.924 --> 00:00:35.721
The lanterns strung over every district are AI.

14
00:00:35.721 --> 00:00:37.288
This lesson opens one up

15
00:00:37.288 --> 00:00:39.902
and names the parts inside.

16
00:00:40.352 --> 00:00:41.780
Start with the engine.

17
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.707
A model is a huge set of numbers,

18
00:00:43.707 --> 00:00:46.052
called weights, saved in files.

19
00:00:46.052 --> 00:00:48.909
A lab trains it on enormous amounts of text,

20
00:00:48.909 --> 00:00:50.778
and runs it on its servers.

21
00:00:50.778 --> 00:00:53.634
OpenAI makes the GPT models.

22
00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:55.515
Anthropic makes Claude.

23
00:00:55.515 --> 00:00:57.198
Google makes Gemini.

24
00:00:57.198 --> 00:01:00.147
At its core: text in, more text out.

25
00:01:00.147 --> 00:01:02.609
On its own, an engine has no seats,

26
00:01:02.609 --> 00:01:05.873
no wheel, and no memory of the last run.

27
00:01:06.322 --> 00:01:07.866
Now build a car around it.

28
00:01:07.866 --> 00:01:10.200
The app is the part you actually touch:

29
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.937
ChatGPT, the Claude app, the Gemini app, Copilot.

30
00:01:14.937 --> 00:01:17.351
It adds the chat window, saved chats,

31
00:01:17.351 --> 00:01:20.382
file uploads, safety checks, and the bill.

32
00:01:20.382 --> 00:01:22.657
And hidden instructions, called a system prompt,

33
00:01:22.657 --> 00:01:24.515
that the model reads first.

34
00:01:24.515 --> 00:01:26.361
One engine can sit in many cars:

35
00:01:26.361 --> 00:01:28.544
the lab's own app, a code editor,

36
00:01:28.544 --> 00:01:30.157
a company's help desk.

37
00:01:30.157 --> 00:01:33.048
And one car can swap engines, with a model picker.

38
00:01:33.048 --> 00:01:35.068
That's why the names blur.

39
00:01:35.068 --> 00:01:37.321
ChatGPT is the car.

40
00:01:37.321 --> 00:01:40.769
GPT, followed by a number, is the engine.

41
00:01:40.769 --> 00:01:42.092
With Claude and Gemini,

42
00:01:42.092 --> 00:01:44.426
the car and the engine share a name.

43
00:01:44.426 --> 00:01:46.063
The numbers change often;

44
00:01:46.063 --> 00:01:48.096
learn the layers instead.

45
00:01:48.547 --> 00:01:51.670
An agent is the same car, allowed to drive.

46
00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.433
It gets tools: it can read your files,

47
00:01:54.433 --> 00:01:57.591
run commands in a terminal, and edit code.

48
00:01:57.591 --> 00:02:00.598
And it works in a loop: decide the next step,

49
00:02:00.598 --> 00:02:02.444
do it, check what happened,

50
00:02:02.444 --> 00:02:05.149
and go again, until it's done or stuck.

51
00:02:05.149 --> 00:02:08.655
Coding agents, like Claude Code, work this way.

52
00:02:08.655 --> 00:02:10.443
Aaron, who made this course,

53
00:02:10.443 --> 00:02:14.913
once wrote Python in Notepad and saved it as .txt.

54
00:02:14.913 --> 00:02:17.595
He didn't know to rename it .py,

55
00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:22.239
to cd into the folder, or what .venv was.

56
00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:24.805
A plain chat window could write that code today,

57
00:02:24.805 --> 00:02:27.162
but the steps around it stay yours.

58
00:02:27.162 --> 00:02:28.787
An agent can take them.

59
00:02:28.787 --> 00:02:30.877
You stay in the passenger seat.

60
00:02:30.877 --> 00:02:33.443
By default, most coding agents ask before

61
00:02:33.443 --> 00:02:36.684
they run a command, and you can say no.

62
00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:39.060
Here's where the picture breaks.

63
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.173
A real engine runs the whole trip.

64
00:02:41.173 --> 00:02:42.590
A model doesn't.

65
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:44.981
With every message, it starts cold and rereads

66
00:02:44.981 --> 00:02:47.582
the conversation the app sends back in.

67
00:02:47.582 --> 00:02:50.728
So the memory lives in the car, not the engine.

68
00:02:50.728 --> 00:02:52.713
Programs can skip the car and reach

69
00:02:52.713 --> 00:02:56.115
the engine through an API, with a key.

70
00:02:56.115 --> 00:02:58.217
That's the engine on a test bench:

71
00:02:58.217 --> 00:03:00.759
no chat window, no saved chats,

72
00:03:00.759 --> 00:03:02.768
unless the program builds them.

73
00:03:02.768 --> 00:03:04.521
And there's no separate driver.

74
00:03:04.521 --> 00:03:07.006
The engine still only makes text.

75
00:03:07.006 --> 00:03:09.932
Some of that text says: run this command.

76
00:03:09.932 --> 00:03:11.859
The app is what actually runs it,

77
00:03:11.859 --> 00:03:14.171
and only if it's allowed.

78
00:03:14.621 --> 00:03:16.862
Four surprises you'll meet early.

79
00:03:16.862 --> 00:03:18.557
Ask two apps the same question,

80
00:03:18.557 --> 00:03:20.600
and you get two different answers.

81
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.202
Different hidden instructions,

82
00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:23.897
sometimes a different engine,

83
00:03:23.897 --> 00:03:26.278
and a little randomness every time.

84
00:03:26.278 --> 00:03:28.356
A program calling the API knows

85
00:03:28.356 --> 00:03:30.573
nothing about your chats in the app.

86
00:03:30.573 --> 00:03:32.651
Same engine, different door.

87
00:03:32.651 --> 00:03:35.902
An agent stops and asks: allow this command?

88
00:03:35.902 --> 00:03:37.760
That's the passenger seat working.

89
00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.838
Read it before you say yes.

90
00:03:39.838 --> 00:03:42.114
And which model is this app using?

91
00:03:42.114 --> 00:03:43.588
Check its model picker;

92
00:03:43.588 --> 00:03:45.423
some apps choose for you.

93
00:03:45.423 --> 00:03:46.619
Don't ask the model:

94
00:03:46.619 --> 00:03:49.430
its answer about itself isn't reliable.

95
00:03:49.879 --> 00:03:52.538
When an agent drives, it crosses the map.

96
00:03:52.538 --> 00:03:53.734
It works in your files

97
00:03:53.734 --> 00:03:55.928
and your terminal: the Machine.

98
00:03:55.928 --> 00:03:59.295
It can save its work with git: the History.

99
00:03:59.295 --> 00:04:02.302
It may hold keys to your accounts: Trust.

100
00:04:02.302 --> 00:04:05.135
AI is the lantern: it lights every district,

101
00:04:05.135 --> 00:04:07.329
but it doesn't walk the streets for you.

102
00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:09.930
Even an agent only goes where you let it.

103
00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.995
And which model tops this month's leaderboard?

104
00:04:12.995 --> 00:04:14.750
Safe to ignore for now.

105
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.779
Three things to keep.

106
00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:18.590
The model is the engine:

107
00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:20.413
a lab's file of numbers.

108
00:04:20.413 --> 00:04:23.803
Text in, text out, no memory of you.

109
00:04:23.803 --> 00:04:25.347
The app is the car:

110
00:04:25.347 --> 00:04:27.936
the chat window, your saved chats,

111
00:04:27.936 --> 00:04:30.235
the hidden instructions and the bill.

112
00:04:30.235 --> 00:04:32.650
One engine sits in many cars.

113
00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.227
An agent is the car you let drive:

114
00:04:35.227 --> 00:04:38.629
tools, a loop, and you in the passenger seat,

115
00:04:38.629 --> 00:04:40.581
deciding what it may do.
